# ===========================================================================
# 🚨 LINE ENDINGS ARE NOT COSMETIC IN THIS PROJECT
# ===========================================================================
#
# tsubasa promises a BYTE-EXACT round-trip: a subtitle read and written with a
# zero shift must come back identical, and `LEDGER.md` records a WebVTT bug
# caused by exactly this — `text.find("\n\n")` does not match `\r\n\r\n`, and
# **66% of the real corpus is CRLF**.
#
# Git's default on Windows rewrites line endings on checkout. If that reached
# a test fixture or a format module, the round-trip suite would pass on one
# platform and fail on another for a reason no assertion names.
#
# ⛔ So: normalise SOURCE to LF in the repository, and mark the data files
# binary so nothing touches them at all.

# Source and docs: LF in the repo, native in the working tree is fine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⛔ NEVER TOUCHED — binary, or byte-exactness is the whole point
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.gz        binary
*.png       binary
*.onnx      binary

# Subtitle files carry their ORIGINAL line endings deliberately. If a fixture
# is ever committed, git must not normalise it — the file's own bytes are the
# thing under test.
*.srt       -text
*.ass       -text
*.ssa       -text
*.vtt       -text
*.sub       -text
*.idx       -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub language statistics — this is a Python project, not a docs project
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
spec/*.md   linguist-documentation
